Well, that's a good way to solve the problem.

00:40:23.333 --> 00:40:26.413
Like, let's get to these things a little bit earlier.

00:40:26.413 --> 00:40:30.374
But but, you know, that that's that was a very, very early days.

00:40:30.374 --> 00:40:32.374
I don't know if, you know, you like that that that happened.

00:40:32.374 --> 00:40:37.454
And maybe I told you this before, but but more recently, these are much bigger questions.

00:40:37.454 --> 00:40:43.134
Like I think I like blows much bigger holes in terms of like how people think about data

00:40:43.134 --> 00:40:46.533
science, about coding, about programming languages, et cetera.

00:40:46.533 --> 00:40:53.054
And so those are much more heated conversations, because in some ways, in some ways

00:40:53.054 --> 00:40:58.614
you're sort of touching on people's self-identity and how they feel about that.

00:40:58.614 --> 00:41:03.413
Right. And you're like, listen, that may not matter anymore.

00:41:03.413 --> 00:41:08.893
I'm not that I'm saying that, but like the implication is that like, boy, you know, does that really matter as much?

00:41:08.893 --> 00:41:12.733
And so people are reacting not so much to what you're saying, but to the implications of what you're

00:41:12.733 --> 00:41:20.494
saying and how and how that affects them and their transition into this new world.

00:41:20.494 --> 00:41:26.814
Are you talking about are you talking about like the kind of shift in what skills are valuable?

00:41:26.814 --> 00:41:28.574
Is that. Yeah, yeah.

00:41:28.574 --> 00:41:35.574
And what does that imply in terms of, you know, product direction, investment levels that we have in certain, you know,

00:41:35.574 --> 00:41:38.413
packages or libraries or approaches?

00:41:38.413 --> 00:41:41.934
You know, how do we shift how do we shift the organization?

00:41:41.934 --> 00:41:50.934
What does that mean? What is the interface that you're going to be using to understand data and to communicate?

00:41:50.934 --> 00:41:56.174
And so, yeah, so that's probably the most recent set of conversations.

00:41:56.174 --> 00:42:03.494
And then I would argue there's probably the heat, the most heated conversations we've had in the last 13 years has been around this.

00:42:03.494 --> 00:42:06.934
Like, hey, what is the what is the future of data science?

00:42:06.934 --> 00:42:09.853
What does that look like? And how do we and how do we reason about that?

00:42:09.853 --> 00:42:14.333
And what is our what is the role that Posit plays in that game?

00:42:14.333 --> 00:42:15.614
It's so interesting to hear.

00:42:15.614 --> 00:42:19.174
And Wes, you do you and Tarif me also.

00:42:19.174 --> 00:42:21.293
Is that your chat?

00:42:21.293 --> 00:42:22.454
We do. We do.

00:42:22.454 --> 00:42:30.733
I mean, I've I've had some type of working relationship with folks at Posit for for going on going on a decade now.

00:42:30.733 --> 00:42:34.174
Like I met I met Joe and JJ when it was just the two of them.

00:42:34.174 --> 00:42:39.614
I think back in 2012, this was even before Hadley joined joined our studio.

00:42:39.614 --> 00:42:51.134
And so I I really identified with the mission of building a sustainable software company that can primarily build open source software for for the public good

00:42:51.134 --> 00:42:59.014
and also solve, you know, enterprise, the enterprise challenges that come along with with doing data science and the enterprise.

00:42:59.014 --> 00:43:11.973
And so, you know, I I got, you know, essentially set up a business partnership with with with our studio before it was before it was Posit to to build

00:43:11.973 --> 00:43:13.333
the Apache Aero project.

00:43:13.333 --> 00:43:18.374
And I was very clear, like, this is going to take a number of years to bear fruit.

00:43:18.374 --> 00:43:31.934
And I think one of the reasons why, you know, I've, you know, been involved with with this company for such a long time is because there's very few companies that that can think and act and operate on such a long time horizon.

00:43:31.934 --> 00:43:46.134
So certainly, like, there's a need to, like, pay salaries and be sustainable and grow and grow the business, but also like to be able to think about projects that are that take a number of years to really fully realize and bear fruit.

00:43:46.134 --> 00:43:53.773
And so it's not just like, what do we need to do to hit like next quarter's numbers or like, you know, the numbers three quarters from now?

00:43:53.773 --> 00:44:04.254
It's like, what, how can we build the right foundation that can sustain us and power us for the next 10 years, the next 15 years?

00:44:04.254 --> 00:44:32.174
And so, you know, I think one of the things that we've, we've often talked about was, you know, early on was like, it's important for us to bring the Python and our data science worlds together and to create have initiatives that help, like, enhance collaboration, enhance interoperability, like to bring these communities together, because I think there was a period in time when when there was this adversarial feeling of like, I'm an R person, I'm a Python person.

00:44:32.214 --> 00:44:38.653
And Hadley and I were always publicly being like, the language wars are stupid and counterproductive.

00:44:39.054 --> 00:44:49.653
But, you know, I think with with Tarif and JJ, like, it's a lot of our collaborations have really been about, like, you know, what can we do right now to to put ourselves on a better path?

00:44:49.653 --> 00:45:08.133
And how can we plan better for for the future so that like, we're in the place that we want to be in two years and in three years and but I really enjoyed like those kinds of like, you know, strategic long term thinking and building really ambitious software initiatives.

00:45:08.814 --> 00:45:17.094
And it's certainly refreshing from having been involved in, you know, other companies in the past where there is like this, the short term, the short term mindset.

00:45:17.854 --> 00:45:28.413
But, you know, I think that there is a need for like, I think in any business partnership or business relationship, there's a need for some amount of like, lateral thinking or like dev devils advocacy.

00:45:28.413 --> 00:45:36.734
So like, if you aren't in some amount of like, questioning the premise, or like, sort of examining like your strategy and saying, like, where are the holes here?

00:45:36.734 --> 00:45:39.334
Like, what, like, like, how could this fail?

00:45:40.133 --> 00:45:46.814
And so it's like, somebody needs to come to the argument, like, sort of with a critical, critical perspective saying, like, what are we not seeing here?

00:45:46.814 --> 00:45:49.334
Like, what are we potentially like, where are blind spots?

00:45:49.334 --> 00:45:51.694
Like, where could our strategy possibly fail?

00:45:51.694 --> 00:45:55.373
Like, what are the things that we could that that could happen that we're not anticipating?

00:45:56.013 --> 00:46:03.493
And so I'm happy that we have things like, you know, DuckDB is one of those things that like came out of seemingly nowhere, not quite nowhere.

00:46:03.493 --> 00:46:05.613
But, you know, we made a huge investment.

00:46:06.854 --> 00:46:14.094
In making DuckDB work really well in both Python and R and like, just magically like interoperate with the things and share data and all that.

00:46:14.094 --> 00:46:18.174
And, you know, it didn't happen by accident, like it required like planning and coordination.

00:46:18.174 --> 00:46:18.893
And, you know,

00:46:18.893 --> 00:46:27.533
Yeah, it's such an interesting partnership, because I do feel like, Wes, like having you around Paws, it's been so interesting to see and useful.

00:46:27.533 --> 00:46:39.613
Just like all the things you're doing and being able to like chat in Slack about even like AgentsVue or RoboRev, all the tools has been super helpful.

00:46:39.734 --> 00:46:45.013
I try not to upset the apple cart, but I'm not, I'm not like known for my, my patience.

00:46:45.054 --> 00:47:11.653
Like I, I, I like getting things, I like getting things done, like as quickly as possible, even if it takes like, you know, if I can get something done in a month, like I'd like to get it done in a month as like kind of one of the, you know, the, you know, the historically Python people, although I'm increasingly a post Python programmer, as one of like the Python people inside Pawsit, it's, it's, I've taken it upon myself to occasionally be the provider of, you know, divergent opinions and whatnot.

00:47:11.653 --> 00:47:13.493
So respectfully.

00:47:14.493 --> 00:47:15.574
Yeah, it seems really important.

00:47:15.613 --> 00:47:17.293
And I, yeah, Tarif, I'm curious.

00:47:17.293 --> 00:47:21.773
I know, Wes, you mentioned like the value of someone playing devil's advocate.

00:47:21.814 --> 00:47:32.373
I think Tarif, you, I saw you mentioned to the importance of devil advocate, devil's advocate, or people feeling like they can argue with you or push back.

00:47:33.013 --> 00:47:36.293
I'm curious as CEO, how often you see that?

00:47:36.293 --> 00:47:39.854
Like, do you feel like people often are willing to push back or?

00:47:39.934 --> 00:47:43.734
There are several people in the company that have no problems at all pushing back on me, right.

00:47:43.734 --> 00:47:45.013
Which I actually love.

00:47:45.054 --> 00:47:51.493
I like, I love the fact that people feel comfortable doing that as opposed to, you know, saying something and believing something else.

00:47:51.974 --> 00:48:04.533
Um, it is also remarkable for me to see how difficult it's been for people to be deprogrammed from other companies that they've been at from like to speak up.

00:48:04.533 --> 00:48:12.814
So we have what we call, um, uh, AMAs, ask me anything that are anonymous, uh, uh, or they can be anonymous.

00:48:12.814 --> 00:48:15.934
Like the default is anonymous and you can sort of self-identify if you want.

00:48:15.934 --> 00:48:21.734
But, uh, and you'd be amazed by some of the things that come up in those questions in those settings.

00:48:21.734 --> 00:48:27.293
And I'm like, listen, why don't you use any of the channels that we have so that we can have a dialogue about it?

00:48:27.574 --> 00:48:29.054
And it turns out a lot of people are scared.

00:48:29.054 --> 00:48:32.653
They're worried that if they were to speak up or if they were known.

00:48:33.334 --> 00:48:36.253
So the problem of communication, you know, it's interesting.

00:48:36.253 --> 00:48:42.934
I've, I've been thinking a lot about this, you know, like how we all are, learn a lot about context for an LLM.

00:48:43.413 --> 00:48:46.214
Well, it turns out the same problem exists with humans.

00:48:46.694 --> 00:48:55.094
If they lose part of the context, then they will give you completely incorrect answers or they make, you know, completely incorrect assumptions.

00:48:55.094 --> 00:49:02.934
And so I, um, you know, I'll go to, to Joe Chang, for example, and I'll be like, you're my chief skeptical officer.

00:49:02.974 --> 00:49:04.974
Tell me what is wrong with my thinking here.

00:49:05.493 --> 00:49:08.054
And sometimes he'll be like, you know, he'll be like, well, I'm worried about this.

00:49:08.054 --> 00:49:08.613
I'm worried about this.

00:49:08.613 --> 00:49:09.133
I'm worried about this.

00:49:09.133 --> 00:49:10.373
I'm like, all right, great.

00:49:10.454 --> 00:49:11.734
Like, I understand.

00:49:11.773 --> 00:49:12.773
I didn't explain myself.

00:49:12.773 --> 00:49:13.413
Well, let me try it.

00:49:13.413 --> 00:49:14.454
How about if I explain it this way?

00:49:14.454 --> 00:49:16.773
It's like, well, if you explain it that way, then I don't have any issues with that.

00:49:17.174 --> 00:49:17.454
All right.

00:49:17.454 --> 00:49:17.934
Well, that's good.

00:49:18.133 --> 00:49:20.094
Sometimes it's like, no, no, I still have these issues.

00:49:20.133 --> 00:49:20.773
I'm like, all right, great.

00:49:20.773 --> 00:49:22.174
Why do we have this disconnect?

00:49:22.454 --> 00:49:24.454
And that allows you to refine your thinking.

00:49:25.214 --> 00:49:28.694
Uh, but there's something tremendously powerful about having people in the

00:49:28.694 --> 00:49:33.253
organization that can use their collective intelligence, uh, to poke holes.

00:49:33.814 --> 00:49:39.013
But also there's a time I found now where like, maybe people won't be able to get there.

00:49:39.013 --> 00:49:44.253
And so there's a, that, that, that puts the onus on you as a leader to sort of say, I get it.

00:49:44.293 --> 00:49:45.653
This is unknowable.

00:49:45.694 --> 00:49:47.413
Like, we don't know what the right answer is.

00:49:47.454 --> 00:49:50.694
It could be this, it could be that, but I'm willing to take the risk.

00:49:50.694 --> 00:49:51.773
Let's go down this path.

00:49:52.334 --> 00:49:57.293
Um, and, uh, and do something because acting like not acting, standing at that

00:49:57.293 --> 00:50:01.533
fork in the road and not doing anything or sort of navel gazing too long is going to

00:50:01.533 --> 00:50:04.214
cause you to lose the game altogether.

00:50:04.214 --> 00:50:08.734
So you're better off take, make the commitment to a path and go and make the

00:50:08.734 --> 00:50:13.293
experiment small enough that if you were to get it wrong, it won't cost you the

00:50:13.293 --> 00:50:18.613
company, but, but, you know, iterate your way, uh, as fast as you can towards the

00:50:18.613 --> 00:50:21.854
answer, which is not always what people want to hear because they're like, you

00:50:21.854 --> 00:50:25.013
know, we're going to, that, that means that you have to accept failure as

00:50:25.013 --> 00:50:26.334
something that is going to happen.

00:50:26.373 --> 00:50:30.974
And, uh, but, but you sort of try and manage that appropriately.

00:50:30.974 --> 00:50:34.054
And for whatever reason, people think that like, if you're the CEO, you're

00:50:34.054 --> 00:50:37.773
not allowed to make mistakes, which doesn't make any sense to me at all.

00:50:37.773 --> 00:50:41.214
Like we're all human beings and we all have imperfect information.

00:50:41.934 --> 00:50:45.214
And so you have to be open to being wrong.

00:50:45.934 --> 00:50:50.533
Um, which is one of the things that really attracted me to, to JJ when I first met him

00:50:50.533 --> 00:50:54.653
was that he was very comfortable with, you know, he's like, here's why I believe

00:50:54.653 --> 00:50:57.373
what I believe, but then if you come in and say, what about this?

00:50:57.373 --> 00:50:57.814
What about that?

00:50:57.814 --> 00:50:59.854
He's like, well, Oh, that's plausible.

00:50:59.893 --> 00:51:00.533
That could be it.

00:51:00.854 --> 00:51:06.413
So the fact that you can be that smart, that capable, uh, and that disciplined

00:51:06.413 --> 00:51:09.854
and yet be willing to reexamine your assumptions when you see new data.

00:51:10.454 --> 00:51:14.174
Uh, I think that is, that's what, that's what you ultimately want.

00:51:14.253 --> 00:51:17.814
And that gives you the best fighting chance of, of, of competing in this world.

00:51:17.974 --> 00:51:19.253
You're so driven.

00:51:19.974 --> 00:51:24.653
Uh, but I'm almost curious, like if we go back to like high school, you know,

